Fictional EP Tracklist

1. Free-Throw Line at 160

Story & Context: During an early rehearsal, Darnell discovers that one heel pattern sounds markedly different when performed directly on the free-throw line because the paint and floor wear change the attack. Asha’s original recording is retained as the basis for a kick-and-clap sequence. Meera answers with one long sarangi phrase that begins before the beat enters and does not resolve until thirty-two bars later.

Niko places only one prepared-vibraphone note at the end of each four-bar drum cell, while Farid sustains a contrabass-clarinet note just below the sub root. Halfway through, the programmed drums disappear and the dancer’s raw heel pattern continues alone beneath the sarangi. When the electronic groove returns, it mirrors the human timing rather than correcting it.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Low-angle documentary view of a worn basketball free-throw line, dance shoes mid-step, sarangi bow hair crossing dark strings in the foreground, prepared vibraphone blurred near folded bleachers, fluorescent sports-hall light. Spoken fragments limited to “one-sixty,” “stay on the line,” “do not quantize,” and “again from the heel.”

2. Rosin on the Center Circle

Story & Context: Meera notices that rosin dust from repeated bowing has fallen onto the painted center circle during rehearsal. Niko uses a soft mallet to tap the floor beside it and records a dull wooden tone that becomes the track’s recurring downbeat marker. The footwork drums are unusually sparse for the first minute: only isolated kick bursts and one displaced clap pattern.

The sarangi melody repeatedly bends toward a stable note without fully landing. Farid mirrors the failed resolution with contrabass-clarinet slides that stop slightly flat. In the peak section, chopped sarangi attacks become part of the drum grid while the original live bow line remains uncut above them, creating two simultaneous versions of the same instrument: one free, one quantized.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Overhead view of rosin dust scattered across a faded center-circle marking on a maple sports floor, sarangi bridge at frame edge, one soft vibraphone mallet on the wood, contact microphone taped nearby, neutral fluorescent light and realistic texture. Spoken material limited to “mark the circle,” “leave the dust,” and “one line stays free.”

3. Bleachers Fold Before the Last Bow

Story & Context: At the end of a late session, a caretaker begins retracting one bank of metal bleachers while the ensemble is still recording. The motor produces a slow mechanical pulse completely unrelated to the 160 BPM grid. Darnell does not sample it immediately; instead, he removes the kick and lets the bleacher rhythm become the only low-frequency timing reference.

Meera plays a descending sarangi line with increasing bow pressure, Niko answers with dry prepared-vibraphone dyads, and Farid uses key clicks rather than pitched clarinet notes. When the bleachers finish folding, the footwork drums return at full speed, making the unchanged tempo feel suddenly accelerated. The final sarangi note continues after the drum cut and ends only when its sympathetic strings stop speaking.

Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Retractable metal bleachers halfway folded inside an empty sports hall, long shadows across the basketball court, sarangi player still seated near center court, vibraphone frame catching fluorescent light, contrabass clarinet silhouette at the far end. Spoken fragments limited to “they are folding,” “keep playing,” “wait for the motor,” and “last bow after silence.”

